"""Reviewable proposals; canonical application is a separate guarded operation."""
import json,copy
from pathlib import Path
H=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
def read(n): return json.loads((H/n).read_text(encoding='utf8'))
O=read('originals.json');S=read('scope.json');G=read('prior_graph_reviews.json')
old=json.loads((H.parent/'microeconomics_wording_graph_cleanup_20261003/expectations.json').read_text(encoding='utf8'))
K={c['id']:c['correctIndex'] for c in old['changes']};P={};R={}
def e(i,stem,good,wrong,feedback,reason,calc='No numerical calculation required.'):
 assert i in S['unique_union'] and len(wrong)==3
 opts=list(wrong);opts.insert(K[i],good)
 P[i]={'correct_index':K[i],'q':stem,'options':opts,'feedback':feedback}
 R[i]={'categories':S['records'][i]['categories'],'scope_reasons':S['records'][i]['reasons'],'original_construct':G[i]['original_construct'] if i in G else O[i]['primarySkill'],'original_skill':O[i]['primarySkill'],'existing_difficulty':O[i]['difficulty'],'reasoning_difference':reason,'noncosmetic':reason,'numerical_reasoning':calc,'difficulty_check':'Preserves '+O[i]['difficulty']+'; '+reason,'four_choice_validation':'PENDING','one_answer_validation':'PENDING','numerical_validation':'PENDING','feedback_validation':'PENDING','hash_validation':'PENDING_APPLICATION'}
 if 'A' in R[i]['categories']: R[i]['duplicate_test']={'repeat_same_reasoning':'NO','reason':reason,'status':'PENDING_REVIEW'}
def g(i,stem,good,other,misconception,third,feedback,reason,evidence,calc=None):
 e(i,stem,good,[other,misconception,third],feedback,reason,calc or evidence)
 slots=[n for n in range(4) if n!=K[i]]
 R[i].update(graph_evidence_required=evidence,economic_reasoning_required=reason,
  graph_test={'answer':'NO','status':'PENDING_REVIEW','economically_plausible_without_graph':['ABCD'[K[i]],'ABCD'[slots[0]]]},
  construct_test={'answer':'NO','status':'PENDING_REVIEW','same_readings_different_economics':['ABCD'[K[i]],'ABCD'[slots[1]]]})
def save():
 for name,data in [('patches.json',P),('acceptance.json',R)]: (H/name).write_text(json.dumps(data,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n',encoding='utf8')

# Duplicate groups: preserve one representative and vary the economic operation.
e('P62C-CPS-L-050','Five buyers value one unit each at $28, $34, $40, $46 and $52. Five sellers can produce one unit each at costs of $45, $37, $53, $41 and $49. The two highest-valued buyers receive units from the two lowest-cost sellers. With no transaction costs, should a third unit be traded?','No. The next buyer values it at $40, below the next seller’s $45 cost.',['Yes. Its $40 value exceeds the $37 cost of the cheapest unit.','Yes. All five buyer values together exceed the cost of three units.','No. The next seller’s $45 cost exceeds every buyer’s value.'],'After sorting values downward and costs upward, the third trade has a net benefit of 40 − 45 = −$5. Two units are efficient.','Tests the extensive margin after efficient allocation, instead of reallocating a fixed quantity between buyers.','Sorted marginal gains: 52−37=15;46−41=5;40−45=−5.')
e('P62C-CPS-L-055','Five buyers value one unit each at $54, $48, $42, $36 and $30. Five sellers’ costs are $39, $43, $47, $51 and $55 per unit. Initially the two highest-valued buyers buy from the two cheapest sellers. An innovation lowers the $47 seller’s cost to $40 and the $43 seller’s cost to $41. With no transaction costs, what happens to the efficient quantity and total surplus?','Quantity rises from two to three; total surplus rises by $4.',['Quantity stays at two; total surplus rises by $5.','Quantity rises from two to three; total surplus rises by $2.','Quantity rises from two to four; total surplus rises by $7.'],'Initially surplus is (54 + 48) − (39 + 43) = $20. After reordering costs to 39, 40, 41, 51, 55, three trades yield (54 + 48 + 42) − (39 + 40 + 41) = $24. The fourth trade is inefficient, so quantity rises by one and total surplus rises by $4.','Re-sorts marginal costs after a selective innovation and distinguishes a cost saving on existing production from a gain from expanding quantity.','Initial TS=102−82=20; new TS=144−120=24; gain4; third gain42−41=1; fourth36−51<0.')
e('42618','X costs $4 and Y costs $8. What is the minimum income needed to afford bundle (2, 1)?','$16.',['$12.','$24.','$48.'],'The minimum income equals the bundle’s cost: 2($4) + 1($8) = $16.','Solves for the income threshold rather than testing membership with income and cost already supplied.','2×4+8=16.')
e('42619','A consumer has $56 and wants to buy 12 whole units of X and no Y. At which listed price of X is that purchase affordable?','$4.50 per unit.',['$4.75 per unit.','$5.00 per unit.','$5.25 per unit.'],'Twelve units cost $54 at $4.50, within the budget. At the other prices they cost $57, $60 or $63.','Solves an inverse affordability threshold for price, rather than dividing income by price to find a whole-number quantity.','12×[4.5,4.75,5,5.25]=[54,57,60,63].')
e('42620','A consumer has $64. Bundle A costs $60 and bundle B costs $68. Which bundles belong to the budget set?','A only.',['B only.','Both A and B.','Neither A nor B.'],'The budget set includes bundles costing no more than $64. A is affordable; B is not.','Classifies two bundles by affordability rather than calculating leftover income.','60≤64<68.')
e('42621','A consumer’s budget line has intercepts of 18 units of X and 6 units of Y. If income is $72, which prices generate this line?','X costs $4 and Y costs $12.',['X costs $18 and Y costs $6.','X costs $12 and Y costs $4.','X costs $4 and Y costs $6.'],'An intercept equals income divided by that good’s price. Thus Px = 72/18 = $4 and Py = 72/6 = $12.','Reconstructs both prices from intercepts instead of writing a boundary from given prices.','72/18=4;72/6=12.')
e('42622','A consumer has $80. X costs $5 and Y costs $8. After buying 8 units of X, what is the greatest quantity of Y the consumer can afford?','5 units.',['10 units.','8 units.','4 units.'],'Eight units of X cost $40, leaving $40 for Y. At $8 each, that buys 5 units.','Uses the remaining budget after a required purchase; the original only checked a supplied bundle’s total cost.','(80−8×5)/8=5.')
e('42623','X costs $6. Buying 14 units of X and no Y leaves a consumer with $4. What income is consistent with this budget?','$88.',['$84.','$80.','$90.'],'Spending is 14 × $6 = $84. Adding the unspent $4 gives income of $88.','Reconstructs income from expenditure and slack rather than rounding an axis intercept.','14×6+4=88.')
e('42625','Income and the price of Y stay fixed. A budget line’s X-intercept falls while its Y-intercept stays unchanged. What caused this change?','The price of X rose.',['The price of X fell.','Income fell.','The price of Y rose.'],'The X-intercept is income/Px. With income fixed, a lower X-intercept means a higher price of X.','Infers the price change from the observed intercept change, reversing the original forward comparative static.')
e('42627','Income and both goods’ prices all double. What happens to the consumer’s budget set?','It is unchanged.',['It expands because income doubles.','It shrinks because both prices double.','Only the X-intercept increases.'],'Doubling both sides of PxX + PyY ≤ income leaves exactly the same affordable bundles.','Tests offsetting proportional changes rather than a single-price pivot.')
e('42645','A consumer strictly prefers A to B, B to C, and C to A. Which diagnosis is correct?','The rankings form a cycle and violate transitivity.',['The rankings violate completeness because all pairs are ranked.','The rankings are consistent if the bundles have different prices.','The rankings are transitive because each comparison is strict.'],'A > B and B > C require A > C under transitivity, contradicting C > A.','Detects an inconsistent cycle among three supplied rankings instead of filling in one missing comparison.')
e('42649','A consumer is indifferent between A and B, and strictly prefers B to C. If preferences are transitive, how must the consumer rank A and C?','A is strictly preferred to C.',['C is strictly preferred to A.','A and C must be indifferent.','Their ranking depends on which bundle costs more.'],'Replacing B with an equally preferred A preserves the strict preference over C.','Combines an indifference relation with a strict ranking, rather than chaining two strict comparisons.')
e('42653','Preferences are complete and transitive. A consumer prefers A to B and A to C. What can be concluded about B and C from this information?','B may be preferred, C may be preferred, or they may be equally preferred.',['B must be preferred to C.','C must be preferred to B.','B and C must be equally preferred.'],'Knowing that both rank below A does not rank them against each other. Completeness requires a ranking but does not tell us which one.','Distinguishes an underdetermined ranking from one implied by transitivity.')
e('42646','A consumer says, “I have compared A and B and like them equally.” Does this response violate completeness?','No. Indifference is a valid comparison.',['Yes. Completeness requires a strict preference.','Yes. Every bundle must have a different rank.','No. Completeness applies only when prices are equal.'],'Complete preferences allow A preferred, B preferred, or indifference for each pair.','Distinguishes a tie from an inability to rank, instead of naming the completeness property.')
e('42650','A consumer ranks every pair of feasible bundles except A and B, saying there is no ranking at all for that pair. Are these preferences complete?','No; at least one pair cannot be compared.',['Yes; almost all pairs are ranked.','Yes; an unranked pair is automatically a tie.','No; completeness requires equal rankings for all bundles.'],'Completeness requires a ranking, including possible indifference, for every pair. No ranking is different from indifference.','Applies the universal requirement to a missing comparison rather than recognizing a fully described property.')
e('42654','A survey records a ranking for every pair of bundles. Is that enough to establish both completeness and transitivity?','It establishes completeness, but the rankings must still be checked for transitivity.',['It establishes both because every pair was compared.','It establishes transitivity but not completeness.','It establishes neither unless every bundle has a distinct rank.'],'Pairwise coverage establishes completeness. The comparisons could still form a cycle, so transitivity needs a separate check.','Separates sufficient evidence for completeness from insufficient evidence for consistency.')
e('42647','Both X and Y are desirable. Bundle A has 6 X and 2 Y; bundle B has 4 X and 5 Y. Does more-is-better alone determine which is preferred?','No. Each bundle has more of one good and less of the other.',['Yes. A is preferred because it has more X.','Yes. B is preferred because it contains more units in total.','Yes. The consumer must be indifferent.'],'Neither bundle dominates the other. The ranking depends on the consumer’s willingness to trade X for Y.','Recognizes the limit of dominance when goods trade off, rather than ranking a dominating bundle.')
e('42651','Both goods are divisible and desirable, their prices are positive, and a consumer can spend all income. A chosen bundle leaves enough money to buy more of either good. Can it be the consumer’s most-preferred affordable bundle?','No. Buying more of a good without giving up the other would improve it.',['Yes. Any affordable bundle is equally preferred.','Yes. Leaving income unspent proves the goods are balanced.','No. An optimum must contain equal quantities of both goods.'],'With more-is-better and no saving option in this two-good choice, unspent income permits a strictly better affordable bundle.','Uses dominance to rule out slack in a choice, rather than merely comparing two supplied bundles.')
e('42655','Both goods are desirable. Bundle E contains 4 X and 3 Y. A proposed bundle D contains 5 X and an unknown whole-number amount of Y. What is the smallest Y amount that guarantees D is preferred to E under more-is-better?','3 units of Y.',['0 units of Y.','2 units of Y.','4 units of Y.'],'D already has more X. At 3 Y it has no less of either good, so it dominates E. With less Y, preferences over the tradeoff are needed.','Solves a minimum condition for dominance rather than evaluating a fully specified dominating pair.','Y≥3; minimum integer3.')
e('42648','A consumer is indifferent between A and B and between B and C, but strictly prefers A to C. Are these rankings transitive?','No. The two indifferences imply indifference between A and C.',['Yes. Strict preference can coexist with indifference for the same pair.','Yes. Transitivity applies only to strict preferences.','No. Indifference itself violates transitivity.'],'Transitivity carries the indifference relation through B, contradicting the stated strict preference between A and C.','Tests a complete proposed set for contradiction rather than supplying the missing tie.')
e('42652','A consumer is indifferent between A and B. A costs $20 and B costs $30. Does consistent preference ranking require the consumer to prefer B?','No. A higher price does not establish a higher preference rank.',['Yes. Spending more always gives more utility.','Yes. Indifference is possible only at equal prices.','No. The lower-cost bundle must be strictly preferred.'],'Preference ranking describes satisfaction with bundles; prices determine affordability. Different prices do not overturn the given indifference.','Separates preference equivalence from expenditure, rather than chaining indifferences.')
e('42660','For the same consumer, two curves are claimed to represent different preference ranks but contain the same bundle. What is wrong with that claim?','The shared bundle cannot have two different preference ranks.',['A bundle’s rank changes with the name of its curve.','Different ranks are possible if the bundle costs more on one curve.','An indifference curve can contain only one bundle.'],'A bundle has one rank for a given consumer. Curves for genuinely different ranks cannot share that bundle.','Explains the shared-bundle contradiction behind nonintersection instead of simply identifying crossing as an error.')
e('42664','Both goods are desirable. One indifference curve lies entirely northeast of another. Which curve represents more-preferred bundles?','The curve to the northeast.',['The curve to the southwest.','Both must represent the same rank.','Their ranking is determined by the numbers of bundles drawn on each.'],'With more-is-better preferences, moving northeast to more of both goods raises the preference rank.','Uses monotonicity to order distinct curves rather than diagnosing intersection.')
e('42668','Curves through bundle A are claimed to represent two different indifference curves. B is indifferent to A on the first, and C is indifferent to A on the second. B has more of both desirable goods than C. Which contradiction follows?','Transitivity makes B and C indifferent, while more-is-better makes B strictly preferred.',['Completeness requires B and C to have identical quantities.','More-is-better makes C preferred because C has fewer goods.','There is no contradiction because B and C have different prices.'],'Both bundles are indifferent to A, so transitivity makes them indifferent to each other. Dominance simultaneously requires B to be preferred, ruling out the proposed curves.','Derives the crossing contradiction from transitivity plus dominance, rather than naming a faulty drawing.')
e('42661','X is desirable but Y is pollution, which the consumer dislikes. If more X compensates for more pollution, how can an indifference curve slope?','Upward: more of the bad requires more of the good to hold satisfaction constant.',['Downward: receiving less of the good compensates for more pollution.','Horizontally: pollution cannot affect satisfaction.','Vertically: the amount of X cannot affect satisfaction.'],'When one variable is a bad, increases in both can offset each other. The usual downward slope assumes both variables are goods.','Changes the desirability assumption and derives the slope, instead of recalling the two-good offset.')
e('42665','Along a smooth indifference curve, the consumer gives up progressively less Y for each additional unit of X. With X on the horizontal axis, how does the curve change as X rises?','It becomes flatter; the absolute marginal rate of substitution falls.',['It becomes steeper; the absolute marginal rate of substitution rises.','It remains straight because utility is constant.','It slopes upward because both goods are desirable.'],'The absolute slope measures Y sacrificed per additional X. Smaller sacrifices mean a flatter curve and diminishing MRS.','Translates changing tradeoffs into local slope behavior, rather than explaining why a slope is negative.')
e('42662','A and B are equally preferred. A consumer strictly prefers the midpoint bundle, containing the average of their quantities, to either A or B. Which standard preference assumption does this observation support?','Strict convexity: a mixture can be preferred to equally ranked extremes.',['Perfect substitution at a constant tradeoff.','A preference for extremes over mixtures.','A requirement that every equal-cost bundle be equally preferred.'],'Strictly convex preferences favor mixtures of distinct equally preferred bundles. Cost equality is not the criterion.','Tests convexity using a concrete mixture comparison, rather than naming a curve from a general taste description.')
e('42666','A consumer is indifferent among A, B, and every mixture of A and B. Does this observation establish strictly convex preferences?','No. Strict convexity would make an interior mixture strictly preferred to the distinct equally ranked endpoints.',['Yes. Indifference along mixtures is exactly strict convexity.','Yes. Every downward-sloping curve is strictly convex.','No. Convex preferences forbid indifference between distinct bundles.'],'Indifference along the connecting segment is consistent with weak convexity, such as perfect substitutes, but not strict convexity between these endpoints.','Distinguishes weak from strict convexity by testing equality, instead of identifying a bowed shape.')
e('42663','Bundles F and G lie on the same indifference curve. F is affordable and G is not. Which statement is correct?','They are equally preferred, but only F is in the budget set.',['G is preferred because it is more expensive.','F gives more utility because it is affordable.','Both must be affordable because they are equally preferred.'],'An indifference curve describes preferences; the budget determines affordability. Equal utility does not imply equal cost.','Separates the preference relation from the feasible set rather than merely reporting indifference.')
e('42667','F and G lie at different points on a smooth, strictly convex indifference curve. Must the marginal rate of substitution be the same at both points?','No. Utility is the same, but the willingness to trade goods can differ.',['Yes. Equal utility requires equal slopes everywhere.','Yes. A curve must be straight if its bundles are indifferent.','No. Different slopes mean F and G cannot have equal utility.'],'An indifference curve holds utility constant, not MRS. Along a standard strictly convex curve, the slope changes with the mix of goods.','Distinguishes equal utility levels from equal marginal tradeoffs.')
e('42673','A utility representation assigns A = 1, B = 2 and C = 3. Which replacement preserves the same strict ranking?','A = 1, B = 4, C = 9.',['A = 3, B = 2, C = 1.','A = 1, B = 1, C = 1.','A = 1, B = 3, C = 2.'],'The replacement must retain C above B above A. Squaring these positive values preserves that order without preserving differences.','Selects a valid alternative representation rather than interpreting one given scale.','1<4<9 preserves1<2<3.')
e('42677','A consumer prefers A to B and is indifferent between B and C. Which ordinal utility assignment represents those preferences?','A = 8, B = 3, C = 3.',['A = 8, B = 3, C = 1.','A = 3, B = 8, C = 8.','A = 3, B = 3, C = 3.'],'A needs a higher number than B, while B and C need equal numbers. The sizes of the gaps do not matter.','Constructs a representation from a weak ranking that includes a tie.')
e('42674','A utility scale gives A = 2 and B = 5. Someone replaces each number with its negative. Does the new scale represent the same preferences?','No. It reverses the ranking of A and B.',['Yes. Any change of numbers preserves preferences.','Yes. Only the size of each number matters.','No. Utility representations cannot include negative numbers.'],'Originally 5 > 2, so B ranks above A. After negation, −2 > −5, so A ranks above B. Negative numbers themselves are allowed; the reversed order is the problem.','Diagnoses an order-reversing transformation rather than confirming an increasing affine transformation.','5>2 but−2>−5.')
e('42678','A consumer strictly prefers A to B. A proposed transformation assigns both bundles utility 10. Why does it fail to preserve the preferences?','It replaces a strict ranking with indifference.',['Utility must be measured in dollars.','Equal utility numbers always imply identical quantities.','Utility numbers must start at zero.'],'A valid representation must retain strict comparisons as well as ties. Collapsing distinct ranks loses information.','Tests the need to preserve strict order, using a non-increasing segment rather than an order reversal.')
e('42675','One ordinal scale assigns A = 1, B = 2 and C = 3. Another valid scale assigns A = 1, B = 4 and C = 9. What does this show about comparing the utility gains from A to B and from B to C?','The ranking is preserved, but the relative sizes of the numerical gains are not meaningful.',['The second scale proves that B-to-C gives five times the satisfaction of A-to-B.','The first scale proves the two gains are equally valuable to the consumer.','Changing the numerical gains must reverse the bundle ranking.'],'Both scales rank C above B above A. Equal gaps of 1 become gaps of 3 and 5, demonstrating that ordinal differences do not measure gains in satisfaction.','Uses two valid representations to test a claim about utility differences, rather than rejecting a ratio within one scale.','Initial gaps1,1; transformed gaps3,5.')
e('42679','A consumer’s ordinal utility rises from 20 to 40 after receiving a good. Can these numbers alone establish that the consumer would pay $20 for it?','No. Ordinal utility differences do not measure willingness to pay in dollars.',['Yes. The numerical increase is exactly $20.','Yes. A doubling of utility means willingness to pay doubles.','No. Ordinal utility cannot rank the two outcomes.'],'The numbers rank the outcomes but do not translate satisfaction into money. Willingness to pay requires information about tradeoffs with money.','Distinguishes an ordinal utility change from a monetary valuation, rather than from a satisfaction ratio.')
e('42676','Person 1 assigns A = 10 and B = 5. Person 2 assigns A = 100 and B = 200. Which comparison is supported?','Person 1 prefers A to B; person 2 prefers B to A.',['Person 2 likes A ten times as much as person 1 does.','Both prefer A because both assign it a positive number.','Person 2’s gain from switching to B is larger than any gain person 1 can receive.'],'Each person’s numbers establish that person’s ranking. They do not provide a common scale for comparing satisfaction across people.','Extracts different within-person rankings while rejecting an interpersonal comparison, rather than explaining unequal numbers for one bundle.')
e('42689','For a consumer with perfect-substitute preferences represented by U = 2X + Y, which bundle is equally preferred to (1 X, 0 Y)?','(0 X, 2 Y).',['(0 X, 1 Y).','(2 X, 0 Y).','(1 X, 2 Y).'],'Both the original bundle and (0, 2) have utility 2. One unit of X replaces two units of Y.','Finds an equivalent bundle from the utility representation instead of naming the preference form.','2×1+0=2=2×0+2.')
e('42691','A consumer treats X and Y as perfect substitutes at a constant rate. With X horizontal and Y vertical, what shape do the indifference curves have?','Straight, downward-sloping lines.',['L-shaped curves.','Strictly bowed curves with a changing slope.','Upward-sloping curves when both goods are desirable.'],'A constant willingness to substitute implies a constant negative slope.','Infers geometric implications from a named preference form rather than inferring the form from a tradeoff.')
e('42693','A consumer treats X and Y as perfect substitutes, with U = 2X + Y. X costs $3 and Y costs $1. With positive income and divisible goods, which spending pattern gives the most utility?','Spend all income on Y.',['Spend all income on X.','Buy equal quantities of X and Y.','Any affordable bundle gives the same utility.'],'X gives 2/3 utility units per dollar and Y gives 1. With perfect substitutes, all spending goes to the higher-return good.','Applies the constant tradeoff to a price comparison and identifies a corner choice.','MUx/Px=2/3<1=MUy/Py.')
e('42695','A consumer treats X and Y as perfect substitutes, with U = 2X + Y. X costs $2 and Y costs $1. If income is $12 and goods are divisible, which bundles maximize utility?','Every bundle on the budget line 2X + Y = 12.',['Only (6 X, 0 Y).','Only (0 X, 12 Y).','Only bundles with equal quantities of X and Y.'],'Each good provides one utility unit per dollar. All bundles spending the full $12 yield utility 12.','Handles the equal-value-per-dollar case, where the optimum is a set rather than a corner.','2/2=1/1;U=2X+Y=12 on boundary.')
# 42697 retained as an instructor-review exception: a sixth introductory treatment would repeat the identification, equivalence or geometry task or require a higher-tier optimization task.
e('42690','X and Y are useful only in one-to-one pairs. X costs $2, Y costs $3, and income is $20. Which bundle provides the greatest number of complete pairs?','4 X and 4 Y.',['10 X and 0 Y.','0 X and 6 Y.','5 X and 2 Y.'],'Each complete pair costs $5. Four pairs exhaust the $20 budget and provide more complete pairs than the alternatives.','Combines a fixed-proportions constraint with a budget rather than naming perfect complements.','20/(2+3)=4 pairs.')
e('42692','X and Y are useful only in one-to-one pairs. A consumer has 4 X and 7 Y and receives one more Y. What happens to the number of useful pairs?','It stays at 4.',['It rises to 5.','It rises to 8.','It falls to 3.'],'X is the limiting good. Extra Y alone cannot create another complete pair.','Identifies the binding good and the zero marginal benefit of an excess component.','min(4,7)=min(4,8)=4.')
e('42694','A consumer uses X and Y only in one-to-one pairs. Where are the kinks of the indifference curves?','On the line X = Y.',['On the line X = 2Y.','Only on the X-axis.','At every point on a straight downward-sloping line.'],'A kink occurs where neither component is in excess: X and Y are equal.','Connects the fixed ratio to kink locations rather than recalling the preference label.')
e('42696','X and Y are useful only in one-to-one pairs. A consumer has 4 X and 7 Y, then loses 2 Y. How many useful pairs are lost?','None.',['1 pair.','2 pairs.','3 pairs.'],'Y falls from 7 to 5, still above the 4 units of X. The consumer can still form 4 complete pairs.','Tests a loss of surplus components and the persistence of the binding constraint.','min(4,7)−min(4,5)=0.')
e('42698','A consumer uses X and Y only in one-to-one pairs and spends $24 on them. Initially each good costs $2. If X rises to $4 while Y stays at $2, how does the number of pairs purchased change?','It falls from 6 to 4; purchases of both goods fall.',['It falls from 6 to 4; only purchases of X fall.','It remains at 6 because Y’s price is unchanged.','It rises from 6 to 8 because X is more expensive.'],'The cost of a pair rises from $4 to $6. With fixed income, pairs fall from 24/4 = 6 to 24/6 = 4, reducing demand for both complements.','Uses a price change to infer linked demands under a fixed-proportions constraint.','24/(2+2)=6;24/(4+2)=4.')
e('42699','A consumer has income $24, Px = $2 and Py = $1. Preferences are smooth, strictly convex and more-is-better. At A = (8, 8), MRS = 3; at B = (10, 10), MRS = 2. A student selects B because MRS equals Px/Py. What is the strongest diagnosis?','B is unaffordable, and A is affordable but can be improved by shifting spending toward X.',['B is optimal because tangency overrides the budget constraint.','A is optimal because exhausting income is sufficient.','Neither bundle is affordable, so no adjustment from A is possible.'],'B costs $30, above income. A costs $24, but its MRS of 3 exceeds the price ratio of 2, so a small move toward more X and less Y raises utility.','Tests feasibility and marginal optimality separately; a matching MRS is not sufficient for an infeasible bundle.','A cost2×8+8=24;B cost2×10+10=30;MRS_A3>2.')
e('42701','A consumer has U = 3X + Y, positive income, Px = $2 and Py = $1. Goods are divisible. A student rejects spending all income on X because MRS = 3 does not equal Px/Py = 2. Which response is correct?','All-X is optimal: X gives more utility per dollar, and an interior tangency condition need not hold at a corner.',['The student is right; every optimum must satisfy MRS = Px/Py.','All-Y is optimal because Y has the lower price per unit.','An equal-quantity bundle is optimal because it balances the goods.'],'For perfect substitutes, MUx/Px = 3/2 exceeds MUy/Py = 1. Utility is maximized at the all-X corner, where equality of MRS and the price ratio is not required.','Diagnoses misuse of an interior first-order condition at a corner, combining preferences and relative prices.','3/2>1/1.')
e('42702','A consumer spends all income at an interior bundle where MRS is less than Px/Py. MRS measures Y the consumer would give up for one more X. Which small affordable adjustment can raise utility?','Buy less X and more Y.',['Buy more X and less Y.','Buy more of both goods without increasing income.','Keep the bundle because every budget-line point is optimal.'],'The market asks for more Y per X than the consumer is willing to sacrifice. Selling back some X to buy Y improves the bundle.','Reverses the marginal imbalance and derives the opposite budget-preserving adjustment.')
e('42703','At an initial interior optimum, MRS = Px/Py. The price of X falls, with income and Py unchanged. At the original bundle, MRS is now above the new price ratio. What follows, assuming smooth, strictly convex, more-is-better preferences but without assuming X is normal?','The old bundle is no longer optimal, but the direction of the total change in X cannot be determined from this local comparison alone.',['The final quantity of X must rise because the substitution effect rules out any offsetting income effect.','The old bundle remains optimal because it satisfied tangency before the price change.','The final quantity of Y must fall because the price of X fell.'],'The old bundle leaves spending capacity and has a marginal imbalance at new prices. The substitution effect favors X, but without restrictions on the income effect the total response is not guaranteed.','Distinguishes a local improvement from a guaranteed final comparative-static result, rather than prescribing a single local move.')
e('42704','At an interior bundle, spending one more dollar on X gives more additional utility than spending it on Y. What small change in spending can raise utility while keeping total spending fixed?','Move one dollar of spending from Y to X.',['Move one dollar of spending from X to Y.','Add one dollar of spending to each good without changing income.','Keep spending unchanged because the prices must be equal.'],'Reallocating spending toward the good with higher marginal utility per dollar raises total utility.','Expresses the marginal allocation test in utility per dollar instead of the MRS/price-ratio representation.')
e('42705','A consumer spends the entire budget and buys both goods. Is budget exhaustion alone sufficient to establish an optimum with smooth, strictly convex preferences?','No. The marginal willingness to trade must also match the market tradeoff at an interior optimum.',['Yes. Every bundle on the budget line is optimal.','Yes. Positive quantities guarantee equal marginal utility per dollar.','No. An optimum always leaves some income unspent.'],'A budget-line bundle can still be improved by reallocating spending if MRS differs from Px/Py. Feasibility and full spending alone are insufficient.','Evaluates a proposed sufficient condition instead of choosing the direction of a known imbalance.')
e('42706','A consumer has a unique optimum with smooth, strictly convex, more-is-better preferences. Income and both goods’ prices then triple. A student predicts more consumption because income rose; another predicts less because prices rose. Which conclusion follows?','The original bundle remains optimal because both the feasible set and the preference ranking are unchanged.',['Consumption of both goods must triple because nominal income triples.','The consumer must move toward X because a higher Px changes relative prices.','The optimum becomes indeterminate because all three nominal magnitudes changed.'],'Multiplying the budget inequality by three leaves the same feasible set. Relative prices and preferences are unchanged, so the same unique optimum remains.','Combines unchanged real constraints with uniqueness and preferences to reject competing nominal-income and nominal-price claims.')
e('42707','A consumer has income $12, Px = $2 and Py = $1. Preferences are U = XY for positive quantities, so MRS = Y/X. Which bundle is the interior optimum?','3 X and 6 Y.',['4 X and 4 Y.','6 X and 0 Y.','2 X and 8 Y.'],'Tangency requires Y/X = 2, or Y = 2X. Substituting into 2X + Y = 12 gives X = 3 and Y = 6.','Solves the budget and marginal condition together instead of giving only an adjustment direction.','2X+2X=12=>X3,Y6;U18 exceeds16,0,16.')
e('42708','Which description identifies a consumer’s optimum among affordable bundles?','A bundle on the highest attainable indifference curve.',['The bundle with the lowest spending, regardless of preferences.','Any bundle with equal quantities of both goods.','Any bundle on the budget line, regardless of preferences.'],'An optimum maximizes preference rank subject to affordability. Low cost, equal quantities or full spending alone does not establish this.','Recognizes the constrained-choice objective rather than applying a local slope comparison.')
e('P62H-MCMP-L-032','A long-run monopolistically competitive firm charges $70, has a $20 markup over marginal cost, and produces 14.64 units less than its minimum-ATC output of 34.64. What are its marginal cost and actual output?','MC = $50; output = 20 units.',['MC = $90; output = 20 units.','MC = $50; output = 49.28 units.','MC = $20; output = 14.64 units.'],'MC equals price minus markup: 70 − 20 = $50. Actual output is minimum-ATC output minus excess capacity: 34.64 − 14.64 = 20.','Solves backward for two underlying firm outcomes rather than calculating the two gaps from supplied outcomes.','70−20=50;34.64−14.64=20.')
e('P62H-MCMP-L-035','At its long-run equilibrium, a monopolistically competitive firm produces 24 units, charges $76, and has marginal cost $49.60. It minimizes ATC at 42.93 units. Free entry has eliminated economic profit. Which conclusion follows?','ATC at 24 units is $76, even though price exceeds MC and output is below the minimum-ATC quantity.',['ATC at 24 units is $49.60 because zero profit requires price to equal MC.','The firm must produce 42.93 units because zero profit requires minimum ATC.','Economic profit is $633.60 because the markup applies to all 24 units.'],'Zero economic profit means P = ATC at the chosen output, not P = MC or minimum ATC. The markup and excess capacity can persist in long-run monopolistic competition.','Integrates zero profit with markup and excess capacity rather than subtracting two pairs of supplied numbers.','(76−49.6)×24=633.6 is not profit;P−ATC=0.')
e('P62H-MCMP-L-038','A monopolistically competitive firm maximizes profit at 18 units and would minimize ATC at 30.12 units. It produces and sells all 18 units. A student calls the 12.12-unit gap “unsold inventory.” What is the best correction?','The gap is excess capacity relative to the cost-minimizing scale, not goods already produced and left unsold.',['The gap proves the firm produced 30.12 units and sold only 18.','The gap measures the difference between price and marginal cost.','Selling all 18 units means the firm must be producing at minimum ATC.'],'Excess capacity compares actual output with the output minimizing ATC. It does not count unsold production.','Diagnoses a stock-versus-capacity interpretation error rather than calculating the gap.','30.12−18=12.12.')
e('P62H-MCMP-L-041','A monopolistically competitive firm charges $82 at its chosen output, where MC = $48.40 and ATC = $82. Suppose a regulator requires a price equal to MC at that same output, holding the displayed costs fixed. Would the firm cover total cost?','No. It would lose $33.60 per unit because the required price would be below ATC.',['Yes. Price equal to MC always covers average total cost.','Yes. Zero profit at the original price guarantees zero profit after the price cut.','No. It would lose $82 per unit because all sales revenue disappears.'],'At the specified output, price would fall to $48.40 while ATC remains $82. The per-unit loss is 82 − 48.40 = $33.60. This fixed-output comparison does not predict a new regulated equilibrium.','Uses the markup in a cost-recovery counterfactual rather than reporting markup and capacity gaps.','82−48.4=33.6.')
e('P62H-MCMP-L-044','Two long-run monopolistically competitive firms charge prices equal to ATC. Firm A has a $17.60 markup and 13.47 units of excess capacity. Firm B has a $12 markup and 20 units of excess capacity. Which comparison is supported?','A has the larger price–MC gap, while B operates farther below its own minimum-ATC output.',['A has both the larger markup and the larger excess capacity.','B has the larger markup because it has more excess capacity.','A must earn more economic profit because its markup is larger.'],'Markup and excess capacity measure different gaps. A has 17.60 > 12 in markup; B has 20 > 13.47 in excess capacity. Both have zero economic profit because P = ATC.','Compares different rankings on two performance measures and separates them from profit.','17.60>12;20>13.47;both P−ATC=0.')

